{"id":"metapool-2025","slug":"metapool-2025","sub":null,"name":"Meta Pool","date":"2025-06-17","one_liner":"Yoink was the first to exploit Meta Pool's mpETH mint() bug (50.77 ETH gross, block 22722394), 50 min before the attacker's first test run and 1 h 44 min before its main tx; it returned its net 45.79 ETH and received a 5 ETH reward.","bot":"Yoink","race_type":"bot-was-first-exploiter","narrative":"Yoink's tx 0x4f43fc6d (block 22722394, index 0, 2025-06-17T06:18:59Z) is the FIRST tx ever to use the mpETH mint-without-deposit bug; the human attacker's first exploit tx (0x691c8070, EOA 0xd08559ce, block 22722643) landed 50 min 00 s later and its main tx (0x57ee419a, block 22722911) 1 h 44 min later. Nothing of the attacker's was pending when Yoink landed: the main-tx EOA 0x48f1d0f5 held only 0.00083 ETH until the first test run paid it 1.64 ETH at 07:08:59Z (its 29.1M-gas main tx needs ~0.03 ETH), the older EOA 0xd08559ce had made no Meta Pool call before 07:08:59Z, and none of the attacker's txs ever appears in the public mempool archive. So the press wording 'Yoink front-ran the attacker' is wrong in the race sense: Yoink independently found/used the bug, the attacker (or whoever built the second tx) came afterwards and reproduced the same call grammar with their own contracts and a Balancer flash loan. Yoink then returned its net 45.79 ETH to the Meta Pool admin EOA and received a 5 ETH reward; the attacker returned nothing.","chain":"ethereum","verdict":"related-not-frontrun","t0_ms":1750141139000,"pre_merge":false,"premerge_note":null,"slot":11943093,"block":{"number":22722394,"hash":"0x6751d14ee3474c297eb5b976b8d1294f87c4adf0c764fd743ccbd7751fdc3ba9","ts_ms":1750141139000,"ntx":176,"builder":"Titan (titanbuilder.xyz)","fee_recipient":"0x4838b106fce9647bdf1e7877bf73ce8b0bad5f97","gas_used":23312185,"base_fee_gwei":0.391251621,"raw":{"block":"raw/rpc/block_22722394.json","receipts":"raw/rpc/receipts_22722394.json","trace":"raw/rpc/trace_block_22722394.json","